\caption(Seven rows in the stated order. The raw predictions remain poorly resolved; the composite's sharper visible regions are copied inputs. Digit order and fixed grayscale are identical in every row.)
\alttext(Ten digit columns compare clean images, masked inputs, raw predictions, explicit visible-copy composites, the fitting mean, hidden errors and zero-code predictions.)

The saved eight-record probe also preserves both attention tensors. Their shapes are
[8,2,4,4] for encoder attention and [8,2,16,16] for decoder attention. Each query row sums to
one within Float32 tolerance. For the first held-out record, the figure below displays both
heads, encoder above decoder, at identical [0,1] grayscale. Encoder cells are enlarged to the
decoder grid size only for display; their numerical matrices remain four by four. Attention
weights describe this execution, not a causal explanation of reconstructed pixels.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/masked_attention.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::map<std::string, n::Tensor> probe;
  for (const auto& [key, value] : n::load_npz("build/masked_probe.npz"))
    probe.emplace(key, value);
  const auto enc = probe.at("encoder_attention"), dec = probe.at("decoder_attention");
  if (enc.shape() != n::Shape{8, 2, 4, 4} || dec.shape() != n::Shape{8, 2, 16, 16} ||
      n::max(n::abs(n::sum(enc, {3}) - 1.)).item() > 3e-7 ||
      n::max(n::abs(n::sum(dec, {3}) - 1.)).item() > 3e-7)
    return 1;
  const auto encoder = n::resize_nearest(n::take_rows(enc, {0}).reshape({2, 4, 4, 1}), {16, 16});
  const auto decoder = n::take_rows(dec, {0}).reshape({2, 16, 16, 1});
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat({encoder, decoder}, 0), "build/masked_attention.png", 2, 0, 1, 1, .8, 12);
  std::print(
      "Every encoder/decoder query row sums to one. First record, head 0 then head 1; encoder "
      "above decoder.\n");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Every encoder/decoder query row sums to one. First record, head 0 then head 1; encoder above decoder.
\image(figures/masked_attention.png)
// result_end

\caption(The encoder attends among four selected tokens in hash order. The decoder attends among sixteen original spatial locations in row-major patch order. The two axes denote queries vertically and keys horizontally.)
\alttext(Four grayscale attention matrices compare the two encoder heads above the two decoder heads; encoder matrices each have four cells per axis, decoder matrices sixteen.)

> Verification and interpretation

The canonical core owns patch conversion, fixed positions, deterministic selection, transformer
components, separate encoding/decoding and masked loss. Python and R use generated native
bindings with thin compatibility facades for remaining examples. The report keeps its complete
editable C++ programs and explicit training loop. Tests exercise all patch/inverse gradients,
Float64 block finite differences, all 35 parameter gradients, malformed selectors, independent
handle lifetimes and the full unsigned seed range. The independent source run retains initial,
first-update, epoch and selected arrays, mask schedules, probe tensors, all-pool predictions
and controls for comparison. Every source array in sixteen report archives matches by dtype,
shape and every value byte; sixteen original archives match the normal Python and R workflows.
An optional independent NumPy oracle (`scripts/check_masked_autoencoder.py`) verifies all 13,649
first parameter derivatives (maximum absolute difference 5.3e-9), bit-exact first Adam updates,
4,600 mask rows and 1,724,800 predicted pixels. The largest prediction difference is 3.8e-7;
code differences are at most 1.1e-6. It also checks attention, hidden-input independence and
visible-order invariance. This development check neither prepares data nor replaces training.

This experiment demonstrates a checked visible-only information path and hidden-pixel learning.
It also exposes the limit of a tiny three-epoch reconstruction model: changing visible content
has little effect on average error, and the fitting-mean control remains essential. More tokens,
depth, training or downstream evaluation would define new experiments. They are not inferred
from this run or from attention images.

> References

- K. He, X. Chen, S. Xie, Y. Li, P. Dollár and R. Girshick (2021/2022),
  [Masked Autoencoders Are Scalable Vision Learners](https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.06377).
  The visible-only encoder and asymmetric decoder motivate this smaller raw-pixel adaptation.
- Y. LeCun, C. Cortes and C. J. C. Burges,
  [MNIST handwritten digit database](https://yann.lecun.com/exdb/mnist/).
  The native preparation manifest records the downloaded IDX sources and selected IDs.
